From: "adnan.drina" <adnan.drina@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 23:33:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] fix(tools): read_file says when it cut a line at the
 per-line cap

RHOAI3 v32: every read_file of a one-line resolved-context JSON
(14.7-35.7K characters) returned ~2.2-2.4K characters with
"truncated": false. The per-line cap (tool_output.max_line_length, 2000 by
default; ShellFileOperations._add_line_numbers, after the shell clamp
`cut -b1-{4*cap+1}` in read_file) left only an inline "... [truncated]"
suffix, and offset/limit page by line, so the rest of the line was
unreachable. Workers turned to execute_code and inline python instead.

When a page contains a line longer than the cap, read_file now probes that
line's full length (LC_ALL=C awk over the page's line range, bytes; one
extra command, only when a line was cut) and reports:

- truncated: true;
- clipped_lines: [{"line", "length_bytes", "shown_chars"}] (up to 50);
- a hint sentence: the line(s) cut at the cap with the full length, the
  "... [truncated]" marker, and that offset and limit page by LINE, so the
  cut part of a line cannot be reached by paging (appended to a pagination
  hint when there is one).

The content is unchanged. A page with no cut line runs the same commands
and returns the same result as before (clipped_lines absent). Local
extension.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
